'''trainable''' (trā′na̤-bl), ''a.'' [< ''train''<sup>1</sup> + ''-able.''] Capable of being trained, educated, or drilled.

{{smaller block|Youth [is] by grace and good councell ''traynable'' to vertue.
{{right|''Lusty Juventus.''}}}}

'''train-band''' (trān′band), ''n.'' [Short for ''trained band'', early mod. E. ''trayned band''; also called ''trained company.''] A force of citizen soldiery identified with London; especially, one company or division of this force. {{smaller|The service rendered by the train-bands to the Parliament during the civil war caused their dissolution by Charles II., but the force was reorganized later, and continued for many years.}}

{{smaller block|There was Colonel Jumper's Lady, a Colonel of the ''Train Bands'', that has a great Interest in her Parish.
{{right|''Steele'', Spectator, No. 376.}}}}

{{smaller block|As to foreign invasion, the Lord Mayor has but to throw himself into the Tower, call in the ''train bands'', and put the standing army of Beef-eaters under arms, and he might bid defiance to the world.
{{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 309.}}}}

{{smaller block|On several occasions during the civil war, the ''train-bands'' of London distinguished themselves highly.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Nugent's Hampden.}}}}

{{smaller|Sometimes used adjectively:}}

{{smaller block|A ''train-band'' captain eke was he
Of famous London town.
{{right|''Cowper'', John Gilpin.}}}}

